You are the fleet QA agent running ONE BATCH gate over FOUR Secuura/Blockchain PRs, THREE TIER 1 and ONE TIER 2, tiered from their DIFFS, all raised by Seat B 40th under Wednesday's brief (two written by the seat, two from local-model goldens): #1330 KS-1352 (the shared VC verifier, packages/shared src/vc/verifier.ts, gains two resolvers keyed by CREDENTIAL ID — `storedRecordResolver` (the issuer's stored record, which POST /api/credentials/:id/revoke writes) and `statusListRevocationResolver` (the status list's own bit, which POST /api/status/:id/revoke writes) — and verify fails when EITHER marks the credential revoked; the status check now runs even when the SUBMITTED document carries no credentialStatus; an id with NO record ABSTAINS; wired by services/vc-issuer src/services/revocationResolvers.ts into POST /api/credentials/verify AND POST /api/presentations/verify; plus a BACKLOG.md entry recording develop's pre-existing packages/shared red), #1332 KS-1054 (migrations/039_rls_fail_closed.sql guards `auth_find_oauth_app_by_client_id … RETURNS SETOF oauth_apps` on oauth_apps existing, and its owner/grant loop skips a function `to_regprocedure` cannot find; api-gateway `runStartupMigrations()` now returns and records `{ ran, applied, failed, lastRunAt, error? }` in services/startupMigrationStatus.ts, which /health AND /health/ready report with their status codes UNCHANGED (Kam ruled a: keep serving, flag it); four existing test annotations widened Promise<void> -> Promise<unknown>) and #1333 KS-1124 F4 (services/originate src/routes/certifications.ts: both certification routes — issue and recertify — save `status: 'failed'` in the blockchain blob when anchoringStatus is 'failed' (Kam ruled card secuura-ks1124-f4-failed-anchor-shows-pending b); the success leg stays statusless pending-onchain; three cells appended to ks543-certify-boundary-strip.test.ts; a Spark golden, the PR == the canonical patch) and #1334 KS-888 (TEST-ONLY, no product change: three cells appended to services/security ks888-failed-mint-save-issues-no-key.test.ts pin that POST /api/keys/validate answers 200 valid from the key itself while its usage write fails, logs ONE error line carrying neither the key nor its hash, and issues the write when it lands — Kam ruled card secuura-ks888-validate-usage-write-failure a; a Spark golden, the PR == the canonical patch). gate37's #1327 / #1328 are MERGED (the merge-base below); develop has since moved by two MERGE commits that are not ours (#1329 KS-1354, #1331 KS-1365). The batch is FROZEN at four; there is NO sibling kit and NO stack. Tier lines: #1330 T1, #1332 T1, #1333 T1, #1334 T2.

WHY THESE TIERS (the drafter's reading of each DIFF against the tiers lesson 0_Brain/learnings/2026-09-05_qa-gate-tiers-and-the-two-nogo-cap.md, not of the titles — grade the tiering itself and say if you disagree): #1330 changes what a PUBLIC verify route answers for a revoked credential (a security surface: a verify that says "valid" is a trust decision), in a SHARED package every consumer of the verifier inherits — T1. #1332 changes a MIGRATION that runs at the gateway's next start on every main database the file stage reaches (039 is the fail-closed RLS flip) and what the liveness / readiness endpoints serve — T1 (schema that deploys; not the data-destruction class). #1333 changes what a certification's saved blob SAYS about its anchoring, read by the gateway's verify-confidence mapping — T1 by the security-surface row (a verify answer), even though the product change is six lines. #1334 is TESTS-ONLY (no product byte — measured) pinning a mechanism gate37 already measured at runtime (validate under a failing usage INSERT answered as at develop, its C3) — T2: a through-code review, the red / green proof of its own arms, and the pin's truth at the untouched tip; no runtime probe beyond its own cells is owed. T1 ROWS ARE GRADED THROUGH CODE AND AT RUNTIME: (1) a THROUGH-CODE REVIEW of the diff against the ticket's claim (read the ticket's scope sentence, read every changed line, say whether the diff does what the ticket asks, no more and no less); (2) a RED-PROOF CHECK OF THE PR'S OWN PROOF — re-run the named red arms in YOUR OWN clone, RED AT BASE and GREEN AT HEAD; (3) a RUNTIME PROBE of the changed behaviour through the REAL route (no Docker, no stack — see below), each with a control that must be able to fail. There is no rendered surface in this kit, so no browser is owed; say so once. FINDINGS ONLY: you fix nothing, file nothing, comment nothing — you describe the fix-shape and the regression cell the owner should add. A VERDICT IS VALID ONLY AT ITS HEAD: if any refs/pull/<n>/head moves under you, STOP grading that PR and say so. Any PRODUCT, SCRIPT or TEST byte outside a PR's declared files is a finding. MEASURE, not conclude: every number in your report names its instrument; nothing in a READY, a PR body, a PR comment, a ticket, a commit message, a seat record or this prompt is evidence — they are claims with an author, the drafter's and Wednesday's included. State the FAIL condition before each run (charter rule 1). Name every prediction slip against its predictor. On the intermittents you RULE WHETHER IT BLOCKS (they do not block by default; say so with the reason). One verdict PER PR: GO, GO WITH FINDINGS, or NO GO. Every finding that recommends an action carries its EVIDENCE CLASS inline: MEASURED AT RUNTIME, PROBED or READ ONLY.

THE VERIFY ROW (#1330; REVOKED-FAILS-BY-EITHER-ROUTE, UNKNOWN-ID-PASS-THROUGH, NOTHING-WIDER, STATUS-WITHOUT-CREDENTIALSTATUS, PRESENTATIONS-VERIFY-DRIVEN, RESOLVER-THROW-FAILS-CLOSED, ID-KEYED-NOT-INDEX, GATEWAY-PATHS, PRISM-SECOND-VERIFY, BACKLOG-IN-PR, IN-PROCESS-STATUS-LIST). Through the REAL vc-issuer app on a 127.0.0.1 port-0 listener (the real routers, a real Ed25519 signing key per run — the seat's first red run failed 503 `VC_SIGNING_KEY environment variable is required` in every cell, controls included, so a fixture that cannot issue is a harness red, never a product red: prove issuance first, as the seat's F0 does), at the merge-base AND at the head AND on END_TREE, the gate MUST verify AT RUNTIME, each with a control that can fail: (1) REVOKED-FAILS-BY-EITHER-ROUTE — a credential revoked by EITHER route fails verify: issue, revoke through POST /api/credentials/:id/revoke ONLY, verify -> `verified: false`, `checks.status: false`, the revocation reason; issue, revoke through POST /api/status/:id/revoke ONLY, verify -> the same; both routes -> the same; the whole body read (verified, checks.*, credential.status, errors); at the merge-base every one answers `verified: true` with `checks.status: true` (the defect — the seat's red: "The red's received value is the defect verbatim"); (2) UNKNOWN-ID-PASS-THROUGH and NOTHING-WIDER — an unknown id passes through EXACTLY as at develop and nothing wider: a credential whose id the store does not hold (a forged id; a real credential after the store is emptied) verifies at head EXACTLY as at the merge-base (whole body), WITH and WITHOUT a submitted credentialStatus; a known, NOT-revoked credential verifies exactly as at the merge-base; and a status-list entry revoked for an id with NO stored record FAILS (the status arm does not abstain on the stored arm's silence) — Wednesday's pass-through is the boundary, and any other change of answer is a finding; (3) STATUS-WITHOUT-CREDENTIALSTATUS — the verifier now runs checkStatus when the submitted document omits credentialStatus (verifier.ts :149-:153, and the no-credentialStatus return :506-:509 now carries the arms' errors): drive a revoked credential submitted WITHOUT its credentialStatus -> fails at head, verifies at the merge-base; (4) PRESENTATIONS-VERIFY-DRIVEN — POST /api/presentations/verify is driven with a revoked credential inside a presentation (by each route) and answers invalid at head, valid at the merge-base; the seat's cells drive NO presentation (READ: ks1352 has F0, R1-R3, C1, C2 over credentials/verify only) — this is the gate's own cell; and say whether presentations/verify's OTHER checks changed (it passes ONLY the resolvers; the constructor's checkStatus default is TRUE, verifier.ts :81); (5) RESOLVER-THROW-FAILS-CLOSED — a resolver that THROWS pushes `Failed to check credential status: …` and fails the credential (verifier.ts :488 / :502, READ): drive `credentialRepo.getById` throwing and say whether that can happen on a live stack (READ credentialRepo getById / loadFromDb) and whether failing CLOSED on a lookup error is inside Kam's ruling (a revoked credential fails) or wider than Wednesday's pass-through; (6) ID-KEYED-NOT-INDEX — both resolvers are keyed by credential id, not statusListIndex (the issue route's module counter vs the status manager's allocateIndex, READ): prove with two credentials whose indexes collide that revoking one does not fail the other; IN-PROCESS-STATUS-LIST — the status list is module state (statusListManagers): a restart or another replica forgets a status-list revoke — say so once, graded as disclosed. The seat's arms (re-run ALL, each line-exact, restored by bytes): a the stored-record arm never reports revoked -> "passed=4 failed=2, RED R1 R2"; b the status-list arm never reports revoked -> "passed=5 failed=1, RED R3"; c both -> "passed=3 failed=3, RED R1 R2 R3" (the seat: "No arm reds a control"); the seat's red at the untouched tip "3 failed / 3 passed (6)" then 6/6; vc-issuer "15 files / 140 tests, 0 failed" (baseline, its file excluded) -> "16 / 146, 0 failed". GATE arms: the widened checkStatus condition put back (:151's two resolver disjuncts removed) -> a revoked credential submitted WITHOUT credentialStatus verifies again; the :509 return put back to `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` -> the same; the presentations wiring (presentations.ts :293) removed -> your presentation cell reds and NO ks1352 cell does (say so: the seat's file does not pin presentations). The RED-ARM ANCHORS (whole lines with indentation; two-line where the text repeats; byte-unique at head and on END_TREE, READ in predict_1.out): verifier.ts :479 (the stored-record arm), :498 (the status-list arm), :151 (the widened condition), :509 after its comment line (the no-credentialStatus return — the same text occurs 8 times in the file); credentials.ts :346 and presentations.ts :293 (the wiring). GATEWAY-PATHS: the api-gateway proxies /api/credentials, /api/status and POST /api/presentations/verify to vc-issuer (proxy.ts :947 / :960 / :970, READ) — drive one verify THROUGH the gateway's route if you can build it without a stack, or say NOT TESTED; the seat left "`/api/batch/verify`: **unread**" and /api/verification/* unread — read both and say whether either verifies a CREDENTIAL (READ: /api/verification goes to originate and verifies DOCUMENTS; /api/batch/verify bulk-verifies document hashes). PRISM-SECOND-VERIFY: services/prism has its OWN `POST /api/credentials/verify` (prism index.ts :754, READ) with its own checks and NO revocation check; the gateway does not route /api/credentials there — say whether it is reachable, and whether it is a finding for this PR or a follow-up. BACKLOG-IN-PR: #1330 carries a +23-line BACKLOG.md entry recording develop's pre-existing packages/shared red — a doc file outside KS-1352's product scope: rule it (a scope finding, or accepted), and note its lines carry foreign hyphenated keys as CONTENT.

NOT COVERED, DISCLOSED (#1330): the pass-through is the OPEN card's default — card secuura-ks1352-unknown-credential-id-verify-policy is STATUS OPEN, ruled_ts null (decisions.json, READ); its option a "Pass-through (being built now)" is the default action; the seat's READY says "Your Q1 ruling **(b) pass-through**" — a letter that names Wednesday's own question, NOT the card's option b (fail closed): say which the code implements (the stored-record arm ABSTAINS on `found: false` — verifier.ts :478-:484, revocationResolvers.ts) and grade pass-through as the disclosed, card-default scope, with its follow-up filed as KS 1368 (Backlog). Also disclosed by the seat: "used its memory fallback throughout" — the DB revoke path (credentialRepo.ts :249-:262) and the swallowed-write path (:78) were not exercised against a database; the status list is in-process; §5f live sweep owed. Kam's ruling on the main card: secuura-ks1352-revoked-credentials-still-verify RULED a (2026-09-28 20:24:15 AEST).

THE START-UP ROW (#1332; FRESH-DB-DRILL, FRESH-DB-039-NEVER-COMPLETES, BOOT-TWICE, GUARDED-SQL-PARSES, HEALTH-BODIES-AS-SERVED, READY-CODE-UNCHANGED, SET-NOT-LATCHED, CORE-THROW-READS-CLEAN, ERROR-FIELD-NEVER-SET, DEPLOY-READS-FAILED, WHICH-FRESH-DATABASES, TS2322-DELTA-ZERO, MIGRATIONS-IMAGE-REBUILD, HEADER-5-21, THE-POSTGRES). The seat NEVER executed 039: "039 IS NEVER EXECUTED." and "The two-sided fresh-DB drill the ticket asks for is NOT done". THE GATE RUNS THE TWO-SIDED FRESH-DATABASE DRILL on a REAL PostgreSQL (THE POSTGRES below), at the merge-base and at the head, through the REAL `runStartupMigrations()` (not extracted SQL): a fresh BARE database (nothing in it — a database the gateway meets without docker/init), boot TWICE on each side; after each boot read `_secuura_migrations` (is 039 recorded?), `to_regprocedure('auth_find_oauth_app_by_client_id(text)')`, the oauth_apps_auth_lookup policy, the tenant_isolation policies' fail-closed shape on the 038 table set, and a call of the function; GUARDED-SQL-PARSES — the guarded DO $guard$ block (nested $ddl$ / $fn$ dollar quotes) parses and runs on PostgreSQL both ways (table absent -> the NOTICE; table present -> the function created, owner secuura_auth_lookup); and a docker/init-seeded fresh database (the compose first start: docker/init/08-oauth-tables.sql creates oauth_apps BEFORE the gateway) booted once — the guard's inert path. FRESH-DB-039-NEVER-COMPLETES — THE DRAFTER MEASURED A REGRESSION (pgprobe_gate38.py -> pgprobe_1.out / pgprobe_gate38.json, PostgreSQL 18.3 over a unix socket, the REAL runStartupMigrations extracted from the scratch clone): on a BARE database the MERGE-BASE fails 039 on boot 1 ("type \"oauth_apps\" does not exist", 7 file migrations failed — the seat's relayed 7), does NOT record it, and applies it WHOLE on boot 2 (function present, owner secuura_auth_lookup, policy present, 48 files tracked); the HEAD applies 039 on boot 1 WITH the guard's ELSE branch, RECORDS it in `_secuura_migrations`, and — because applyFileMigrations never re-runs a recorded file (startup-migrations.ts :133-:137, READ) — boot 2 STILL has NO auth_find_oauth_app_by_client_id and NO oauth_apps_auth_lookup policy (a call answers 42883), for good; the head's NOTICE text says "the next boot creates the function" — it does not; the head's boot-2 summary reads `{ ran: true, applied: 45, failed: 0 }` — /health would read CLEAN over a database 039 never completed. The only callers are the OAuth token paths (services/auth src/services/oauth.ts :133, :160 — the pre-auth client lookup). A drafter's run, NOT your evidence: RE-MEASURE it, both sides, and RULE it: the head trades the base's boot-1 fail-OPEN window (which the ticket names) for a PERMANENT loss of the OAuth client lookup (and its policy) on every bare database — say whether that is a NO GO, and name the fix-shape (e.g. record 039 only when the guarded function exists, move the function to a file that runs after CORE, or have CORE create oauth_apps's function). WHICH-FRESH-DATABASES — say which deployed databases the gateway meets WITHOUT docker/init (a managed Azure Postgres? a restored one?) and so which environments the regression reaches; the per-tenant stage runs NO file migrations (KS-1055, READ). HEALTH-BODIES-AS-SERVED — the /health and /health/ready bodies AS SERVED: build the real api-gateway health routes (createHealthRoutes, health.ts) on a 127.0.0.1 port-0 listener in YOUR OWN process, record a FAILED run and a CLEAN run through the real `recordStartupMigrations`, and read both bodies and both status codes: `ran: false` before any run (never `failed: 0` as though a run happened); a failed run -> /health 200 `healthy` with `startupMigrations.failed > 0`; /health/ready's code unchanged by the flag (READY-CODE-UNCHANGED: `res.status(ready ? 200 : 503)`, health.ts :191, reads dependencies only — the seat's H4 "was asserting the harness" until rewritten two-sided); SET-NOT-LATCHED — a failing run then a clean run reads clean ("RED H2 H3 H4 H5" is the seat's latch arm). CORE-THROW-READS-CLEAN — the file-stage catches add `totalFailed += 1` (:1010, :1025) but the CORE-stage catches (main :1093, platform :1194) do NOT: drive a CORE stage that THROWS and read the recorded `failed`; ERROR-FIELD-NEVER-SET — `error?` ("Present only when a stage threw") is never passed to recordStartupMigrations (:1272 passes applied / failed only): say whether the declared field is dead. DEPLOY-READS-FAILED — Kam's option a says "the deploy scripts' existing /health checks … see it and the deploy reads as failed": grep deploy-all.sh :281 (ruled OUT OF SCOPE) and deploy.sh :823 at head — does any deploy read `startupMigrations.failed` yet? TS2322-DELTA-ZERO — "TS2322 ×4": type-check api-gateway with a config that extends its tsconfig with `exclude: []` at the launch develop, the head and END_TREE, list the program, compare the whole ERROR SET (the seat: "29 — identical to the pristine tip"): the including-program delta is zero, and none of the 29 is in a touched file. MIGRATIONS-IMAGE-REBUILD — the seat: 039 is "BAKED into the `migrations` image" — say what a deploy must rebuild. HEADER-5-21 — both READYs say the decision is recorded in "the :5-21 header": 039's diff touches ONLY :221-:257 and :271-:281 (039 :1-:24 is KS-458's header, byte-unchanged); startup-migrations.ts, whose own header :5-:21 describes the stage order, gained the KS-1054 record at :23-:42 — say which file the brief meant and whether the record is where it was asked for. The seat's arms (re-run ALL, line-exact, restored by bytes): A the 039 table guard removed -> RED S1 only; B the owner/grant guard removed -> RED S2 only; C the recorder LATCHES -> "RED H2 H3 H4 H5"; D /health drops the field -> RED H0 H1 H2 H3 H5; E /health/ready drops the field -> RED H4 only; its red at the untouched tip "8 failed / 1 passed (9)" (S1's red: "expected [ 'oauth_apps' ] to deeply equal []"), then 9/9; api-gateway "86 files / 781 tests, 0 failed" -> "87 / 790, 0 failed". GATE arms on the REAL Postgres: arm A (the guard removed) -> the head's bare boot 1 fails 039 again exactly as the base; arm B -> the loop fails on the absent function. The RED-ARM ANCHORS (READ): 039 :243 (`EXECUTE $ddl$`, inside the guard) and :278 (the to_regprocedure guard); startup-migrations.ts :1272 (the one record); health.ts :94 and :199 (the same text twice: plant by the TWO-LINE anchor with its comment line).

THE POSTGRES (#1332): the drafter MEASURED on a throwaway Homebrew PostgreSQL 18.3 (pgprobe_gate38.py: initdb-18 into the scratchpad, `pg_ctl-18 … -o "-c listen_addresses='' -k <sock> -p 55438"` — the port only names the socket file; `SHOW listen_addresses` read '' and lsof read 0 inet sockets on the postmaster; stopped with `pg_ctl-18 -m fast stop`, the data dir kept), the REAL runStartupMigrations run by node with the checkout's tsx CJS hook (the tsx CLI refused: it opens an IPC pipe in TMPDIR and a scratchpad TMPDIR is too long for a unix socket — MEASURED, listen EINVAL). If no Postgres is available to you, SAY SO PLAINLY and mark every item that needed it NOT TESTED. Use gate37's gate method (its report, THE ROTATION TABLE): `-c listen_addresses=''` (NO TCP listener) and `-k <dir>` with a socket directory shorter than 103 bytes including `/.s.PGSQL.<port>` (a fresh short `mktemp -d`), stopped with `pg_ctl-18 -m fast stop` when you are done, its data directory quarantined, never deleted. NEVER the native Postgres on :5432, never a container. The repo carries two real-Postgres shell suites you may run standalone in your worktrees (both start their own throwaway cluster on a socket): scripts/__tests__/ks949_main_seed_idempotence.test.sh (applies docker/init + migrations/*.sql, and needs 039 — READ :35-:36) and migrations/__tests__/ks667-044-scenarios.sh (the executed-migration scenario pattern, a model for a 039 scenario). Say which engine and version each number came from.

THE CERTIFY ROW (#1333; CERTIFY-BOTH-ROUTES, READY-IDENTITY, GIT-APPLY-NOT-PATCH, FAILED-LITERAL-READERS, HERMETIC-KS1293, LOCAL-MODEL-PROVENANCE). READY-IDENTITY: the PR diff EQUALS the canonical patch — the drafter MEASURED the READY's diff block == the brief's golden (briefs/KS-1124-F4/KS-1124.golden.diff) == the Spark checker's patch.diff (runs/spark_secuura_2026-09-28_KS-1124-F4/out.md.checker/patch.diff), and `git apply --cached --check` then apply, STRICT, at the merge-base gives EXACTLY the head's blobs for both paths (diff(golden-applied, head) EMPTY) — re-verify with `git apply --check`, never `patch -F0` (GIT-APPLY-NOT-PATCH). CERTIFY-BOTH-ROUTES — both certification routes save status failed when anchoring failed: through the REAL originate app (port 0; the anchoring service unreachable, then a stub that accepts), POST /api/certifications/issue and POST /api/certifications/:id/recertify, read the SAVED blob (the repository's save argument, or a real store if you can build one): anchoring failed -> `status: 'failed'`, `anchoringStatus: 'failed'`, `anchorId: null`, `confidence` still 'pending-onchain'; anchoring accepted -> NO `status` key, `confidence: 'pending-onchain'`, the anchor id — at the merge-base the failed leg saves NO status (the defect). FAILED-LITERAL-READERS — who reads the new literal: originate verification.ts :330 and verificationV2.ts :131 / :401 read `failed || anchor_failed` as failed; documents.ts :1098 / :1532 (anchored) and :1338 (the retry guard) read ONLY `anchor_failed`, so a bare 'failed' reads ANCHORED and NOT retryable there (the READY and the body: "Nothing gets worse." — the blob had no status before); drive what the api-gateway's verify-confidence mapping serves for a certification saved 'failed' (the gateway's verification.ts ~:232, ks1071's one mapping) — served, not read — and say whether a CERTIFICATION blob ever reaches documents.ts's readers. The body: "arm D pins that choice" (the literal 'failed', not 'anchor_failed'). HERMETIC-KS1293: the cells were APPENDED to ks543 because a new file setting ANCHORING_SERVICE_URL "reddens ks1293's hermetic gate" (the body) — run ks1293-originate-suite-is-hermetic at the head and END_TREE. The checker's figures ("2 failed / 5 run" red-first, then 5/5; originate jest, no new red; tsc rc 0) and the seat's (STATUS mail: originate 89 suites / 1055 -> 89 / 1058; arms A -> F4-1 only, B -> F4-2 only, C -> F4-3 only, D ('anchor_failed' instead of the ruled literal) -> F4-1 only; the two tamper lines are byte-identical, selected by line number) are claims — re-run them. Wednesday asks you to CONFIRM the seat's reading of the readers and JUDGE documents.ts's anchor_failed-only reads PRE-EXISTING (the tip saved no status at all) unless you measure otherwise. Kam's ruling: card secuura-ks1124-f4-failed-anchor-shows-pending RULED b ("secuura-ks1124-f4-failed-anchor-shows-pending = b", the READY). LOCAL-MODEL-PROVENANCE: grade the code on its merits; provenance earns no credit and no penalty. The RED-ARM ANCHORS (READ): certifications.ts :474 (issue) and :1212 (recertify) — the SAME text twice: plant by the TWO-LINE anchor with each comment line.

THE PIN ROW (#1334; PIN-GREEN-OR-RED-AT-TIP, PIN-ARMS-ON-PRODUCT, VALIDATE-UNCHANGED, STALE-REPLICA-UPSERT, SECURITY-COUNTS). READY-IDENTITY: the PR diff EQUALS its canonical patch — the drafter MEASURED the READY's diff block == the golden (briefs/KS-888-validate-logonly/KS-888.golden.diff) == the checker's patch.diff, and the strict `git apply --cached` at the merge-base gives the head's blob exactly. PIN-GREEN-OR-RED-AT-TIP — the claims CONTRADICT: the PR body says the cells are GREEN at the untouched tip by design ("TEST-ONLY. No product change."; validate already logs-only: index.ts :1369 takes no opt-in, :332 logs one line, :336 rethrows only on opt-in), the checker's verdict says "RED-FIRST: … fails at the untouched tip (1 failed / 19 run; controls green; assertion reds)" — run the head's ks888 file against the MERGE-BASE product (and the head's own) and say whether its cells are GREEN or RED at the untouched tip, naming any red cell and its assertion; a pin that is red at the tip asserts behaviour the product lacks and is not a pin. PIN-ARMS-ON-PRODUCT — all the discrimination is in the arms planted on the PRODUCT file in YOUR worktree (the seat's STATUS mail: A key hash added, B log line deleted, C log doubled -> V2 only each; D the REFUSE shape Kam ruled OUT -> 5 red: V1 x3, V2 and the existing C3, by assertion; security 26 files / 270 -> 26 / 275): each must red exactly those cells and restore by bytes — Wednesday: arm D MUST red 5 cells; anchors index.ts :332 (the log line) and :1369 (validate's write), both byte-unique (READ). VALIDATE-UNCHANGED — no product byte (measured); security's validate answers at head exactly as at develop. STALE-REPLICA-UPSERT — the seat measured it (source path, NOT driven) and filed KS-1370 (High): validate resolves the key from a boot-time memory map (index.ts :1326-:1358), so a revoked key may keep authenticating in a stale process, and the usage upsert (:1369 -> :316-:318) writes is_active from that stale copy back over a